Use RefactorConstants.h.pkg
Use cLibraryInventory.pkg
Use cShadowedPackageFiles.pkg    // OrphanedLibraryFolders - the library folders a package replaced
Use RDCRunProgram.pkg          // RDCOpenUrl - the 'Open Containing Folder' button
Use FTMarkupBuilders.pkg         // the HTML vocabulary - OpenHtml, IconLabel, the builders
Use FTDashIcons.pkg              // DashIcon - the inline SVG glyphs
Use RefactorFileIO.pkg           // rfOpenOutput / rfCloseOutput - channel file I/O
Use cEncodingHelpers.pkg         // rfHtmlEscape and the encoding helpers
Use RefactorConstants.h.pkg      // CS_SpanEnd / CS_DivEnd - the closing-tag constants
Use OutputDockFT.dg              // the analyzer grid of ghoModAnalyzerDock - LibraryMessageCounts reads its messages, so the dock is defined before this compiles

/*  cFTLibraryReport - the shared-library DATA behind the Libraries tile: the tile's own counts, the compiler-message tally per library, the dependency scan, and the
    HTML fragments a screen builds its rows from.

    THE REPORT ITSELF MOVED OUT, 2026-08-11. It lived here as a ghoConfirmDialog body - one body and
    two chips - beside a separate Library Maintenance dialog that edited the same [Libraries] list.
    Two surfaces over one subject, reached one after the other, and reading DIFFERENT SOURCES: this
    one dropped every entry whose .sws did not resolve, so it could not show the rows most in need of
    an edit. They are now one dialog (MaintainLibrariesFT.dg), built on the raw entries, and the tile
    opens it directly. What stayed here is everything that is data rather than screen.

    LIFTED OUT OF THE DASHBOARD VIEW (2026-07-31). The guided trail borrowed seven members from
    oDashboard_vw - tile markup, this report, and the Deep Scan driver itself - so the old Dashboard
    was not a peer screen but the trail's implementation host, and nothing could be stripped from it
    without risking the guided flow. This is the first slice moved out.

    Its OWN package rather than a shared dumping ground: the library readout belongs with the
    library code, the same way the scan driver belongs with the scanner. Same de-coupling, better
    result.

    A class plus one global object, so either view calls it directly instead of through the other.
*/
/*  cLibraryInventoryApp - cLibraryInventory with the JSON hooks filled in.

    THE DOCUMENTED ESCAPE HATCH, taken. cLibraryInventory leaves SwsJsonVersion and
    SwsJsonLibraryEntries as stubs on purpose: parsing a DF26 JSON .sws needs cJsonObject and the
    reader that owns it (cRefactorApplication.ReadSwsJsonFile), and a ghoApplication reference in
    that class would make it unusable from the console test runner, which declares a plain
    cApplication - every call would return nothing, silently. So the base class stays testable and
    the app-side subclass supplies the half that needs the app.

    CREATE THIS, NOT cLibraryInventory, anywhere inside the FT application. A plain cLibraryInventory
    still works and simply reports a JSON workspace as having no libraries - which is why every
    creation site in this package and in MaintainLibrariesFT.dg names this class instead.

    PER FILE, NEVER PER WORKSPACE (Nils 2026-08-12). A JSON workspace routinely references libraries
    whose own .sws files are still INI - the DF26 Studio converts the workspace it migrates and
    leaves the libraries alone (verified 2026-08-05: 1 JSON against 103 INI). Both overrides
    therefore test the FILE they were handed, and the INI readers in the base class go on serving
    every library that is still INI. Mixed workspaces are the normal case, not an edge one.
*/
Class cLibraryInventoryApp is a cLibraryInventory

    // The "df" member of a JSON .sws - the JSON counterpart of [Properties] Version=. Guarded on
    // existence because ReadSwsJsonFile pops an Info_Box on a missing file, and this is asked for
    // every library a workspace declares, including the ones that are not there.
    Function SwsJsonVersion String sSwsFile Returns String
        tSwsFile Sws
        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return ""
        If (SwsFormatOf(Self, sSwsFile) <> eSwsJson) ;
            Function_Return ""
        Get ReadSwsJsonFile of ghoApplication sSwsFile to Sws
        Function_Return Sws.sDfVersion
    End_Function

    /* The "dependencies" array as plain entry strings - the JSON counterpart of [Libraries].

       OBJECT-FORM DEPENDENCIES ARE SKIPPED HERE, and the writer refuses any file that has one, so
       the two halves agree: what cannot be shown as a path is never silently rewritten as one. A
       git/cloud dependency has no path to resolve, no version to read off disk and no folder to
       scan for siblings - listing it would produce a row that is "not found" for the wrong reason.

       Paths come back with forward slashes, as df-cli writes them. Nothing converts them:
       NormalizePath folds "/" to "\" before every comparison, and _ResolvePath collapses the
       leading "../" through the same call - so a JSON entry resolves exactly like an INI one. */
    Function SwsJsonLibraryEntries String sSwsFile Returns String[]
        tSwsFile Sws
        String[] asOut
        String sPath
        Integer i iMax

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return asOut
        Get ReadSwsJsonFile of ghoApplication sSwsFile to Sws
        Move (SizeOfArray(Sws.aDependencies) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Sws.aDependencies[i].bIsObject = False) Begin
                Move (Trim(Sws.aDependencies[i].sPath)) to sPath
                If (sPath <> "") ;
                    Move sPath to asOut[-1]
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return asOut
    End_Function

    /* The df-min / df-max a package declares in its own df.sws - the app-side half of the hook.

       Guarded on existence AND on the file being JSON: a package's manifest always is, and
       ReadSwsJsonFile pops an Info_Box on a missing file, which this must never do - it is asked
       once per package row, including rows whose cache entry is absent. */
    Procedure SwsPackageDfRange String sSwsFile String ByRef sMin String ByRef sMax
        tSwsFile Sws

        Move "" to sMin
        Move "" to sMax
        If (sSwsFile = "") ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Procedure_Return
        If (SwsFormatOf(Self, sSwsFile) <> eSwsJson) ;
            Procedure_Return
        Get ReadSwsJsonFile of ghoApplication sSwsFile to Sws
        Move Sws.Package.sDfMin to sMin
        Move Sws.Package.sDfMax to sMax
    End_Procedure

    // The git/file OBJECT dependencies this workspace declares - the ones SwsJsonLibraryEntries
    // leaves out. Counted so a screen can say they exist rather than quietly listing fewer rows
    // than the file has.
    Function SwsJsonObjectDependencyCount String sSwsFile Returns Integer
        tSwsFile Sws
        Integer i iMax iCount

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return 0
        If (SwsFormatOf(Self, sSwsFile) <> eSwsJson) ;
            Function_Return 0
        Get ReadSwsJsonFile of ghoApplication sSwsFile to Sws
        Move 0 to iCount
        Move (SizeOfArray(Sws.aDependencies) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Sws.aDependencies[i].bIsObject = True) ;
                Increment iCount
        Loop
        Function_Return iCount
    End_Function

    /* The object dependencies themselves, in file order.

       sName is what df-cli calls the package - the key "package uninstall" wants - derived as
       <owner>/<sws stem> from the source url and the sws path, which is how df-cli named
       https://github.com/NilsSve/Library-DUF.git + DUFLibrary26.0.sws as NilsSve/DUFLibrary26.0.
       A DERIVATION, and stated as one: df-cli config knows the real names, so if a repository is
       ever laid out in a way this gets wrong, that is where to read them from instead. */
    Function SwsJsonObjectDependencies String sSwsFile Returns tSwsDependency[]
        tSwsFile Sws
        tSwsDependency[] aOut aNone
        Integer i iMax iRow

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return aNone
        If (SwsFormatOf(Self, sSwsFile) <> eSwsJson) ;
            Function_Return aNone
        Get ReadSwsJsonFile of ghoApplication sSwsFile to Sws
        Move (SizeOfArray(Sws.aDependencies) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (Sws.aDependencies[i].bIsObject = True) Begin
                Move (SizeOfArray(aOut)) to iRow
                Move Sws.aDependencies[i] to aOut[iRow]
            End
        Loop
        Function_Return aOut
    End_Function

    // The workspace overrides, as read from the JSON. Guarded on existence and on format for the
    // same reason as its neighbours: ReadSwsJsonFile pops an Info_Box on a missing file.
    Function SwsJsonOverrides String sSwsFile Returns tSwsOverride[]
        tSwsFile Sws
        tSwsOverride[] aNone

        If (vFilePathExists(sSwsFile) = 0) ;
            Function_Return aNone
        If (SwsFormatOf(Self, sSwsFile) <> eSwsJson) ;
            Function_Return aNone
        Get ReadSwsJsonFile of ghoApplication sSwsFile to Sws
        Function_Return Sws.aOverrides
    End_Function
End_Class

// From here on the inventory created through giLibraryInventoryClass reads a JSON workspace file too
// (see the note at that variable, cLibraryInventory.pkg).
Move (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to giLibraryInventoryClass

Class cFTLibraryReport is a cObject

    Procedure Construct_Object
        Forward Send Construct_Object
        // Memo for the tile half - see RefreshLibraryCounts. Keyed "<lowercased .sws>|<target
        // version>" so it self-invalidates when either changes.
        Property String  psLibCountKey  ""
        // True when the .sws is DF26 JSON. Kept from the count pass so the sub-line can drop the
        // version talk on a workspace where it means nothing - see LibraryTileHtml.
        Property Boolean pbLibJsonForm  False
        Property Integer piLibTotal     0
        Property Integer piLibAttention 0
        // Git / cloud PACKAGES. Counted apart from piLibTotal because they are a different
        // population: they carry no .sws version this tile can judge, so they belong in what the
        // workspace HAS, never in the denominator of what needs a build. See LibraryTileHtml.
        Property Integer piLibPackages  0
        /* THE LIBRARY FOLDERS A PACKAGE REPLACED that are still on disk (Nils 2026-09-10, after
           migrating a workspace from libraries to packages: "it would be more logical to have this
           on the Libraries tile"). Counted inside RefreshLibraryCounts, so it rides that memo -
           whose key carries the .sws MTIME, which is exactly what moves when libraries are swapped
           for packages. The folders themselves are kept for the dialog's Retire folders button. */
        Property Integer  piLibStale    0
        Property String[] pasLibStaleFolder
        /* Usage results for the CURRENT report showing only. ShowLibraryReport re-enters itself once
           with these set, so the row loop has one place to read them from and the report has one
           renderer instead of two that could drift. Deliberately not cached across openings: they
           describe source that a refactoring run may have rewritten in between, and a stale
           dependency count reads exactly like a fresh one. */
        Property tLibraryUsage[] pUsages
        Property Boolean         pbHaveUsages False
        /* THE UPDATE ANSWER, pushed in from OUTSIDE rather than fetched here (2026-09-10).

           WorkspaceUpdateScan lives in cDfCliPackages.pkg, which Uses THIS file - so this class
           cannot call it without a circular Use. That is not merely a workaround: the scan spawns
           processes and touches the network, and a tile BUILDER that did any of that would run it
           on every repaint. The scan is arranged by the boards, once, and leaves its verdict here
           for the builder to read.

           psUpdScanKey IS THE WORKSPACE THE ANSWER IS ABOUT, and LibraryTileHtml refuses an answer
           that does not name the workspace it is painting. Loading a second workspace would
           otherwise show the first one's verdict until the new scan finished.

           pbUpdAnswered is separate from piUpdBehind because nothing-behind and could-not-check
           are the same number and must never become the same sentence. */
        Property String  psUpdScanKey  ""
        Property Integer piUpdBehind   0
        Property Boolean pbUpdAnswered False
    End_Procedure

    /* Record what a scan found. sSwsFile is what the answer is ABOUT, not merely context - see
       psUpdScanKey. bAnswered False parks a "we asked and nothing could tell us" result, which
       stops the board asking again this session while leaving the tile silent. */
    Procedure SetUpdateResult String sSwsFile Integer iBehind Boolean bAnswered
        Set psUpdScanKey  to (Lowercase(Trim(sSwsFile)))
        Set piUpdBehind   to iBehind
        Set pbUpdAnswered to bAnswered
    End_Procedure

    /*  FORGET THE ANSWER - the dependency array it described has moved.

        Clears the KEY, not just the numbers, and that distinction is the whole point: a key left
        naming this workspace would make UpdateScanDone True, the boards would decline to re-arm,
        and the tile would stay silent about updates for the rest of the session. Cleared, the
        next board refresh arms a fresh scan by itself. A dependency change is exactly when
        re-asking is worth the round trip.

        Callers that know MORE than "something moved" should follow this with SetUpdateResult -
        an upgrade knows precisely which row went current and can restate the count rather than
        pay for a scan to rediscover it. */
    Procedure InvalidateUpdateResult
        Set psUpdScanKey  to ""
        Set piUpdBehind   to 0
        Set pbUpdAnswered to False
    End_Procedure

    // Has this workspace been scanned already? The boards ask before arming, so opening the same
    // workspace twice in one session costs one scan and not two.
    Function UpdateScanDone String sSwsFile Returns Boolean
        String sKey sSeen
        Move (Lowercase(Trim(sSwsFile))) to sKey
        Get psUpdScanKey to sSeen
        Function_Return (sKey <> "" and sKey = sSeen)
    End_Function

    /* WHO DEPENDS ON WHAT: every DIRECT Use of a library's packages, across the workspace's own
       source. The report can say "no 26.0 build - needs a newer copy" without the user having any
       idea what that costs them; this is the answer to "how much of my code cares?", and 3 files
       versus 300 is the difference between a swap and a project.

       DIRECT ONLY (Nils 2026-08-01). A file that Use's vwin32fh itself is the user's problem; one
       that reaches it through VDFQuery is VDFQuery's, and folding the two together would overstate
       the work AND point at files the user cannot usefully change.

       THE LIBRARY'S OWN SOURCE IS EXCLUDED - a library's packages Use each other constantly, and
       counting that as "usage" would report a library as heavily depended upon by nobody but itself.
       Files are dropped by folder prefix, which is exact: the inventory already resolved every
       library's folder.

       Runs on demand from the report, never on the tile paint: this reads every source file in
       scope, which is Deep-Scan-sized work and has to be asked for. Panel-driven and cancellable on
       the same gate the scan uses (Check_StatusPanel; the panel's Cancel does not close it). */
    /* One library's dependency summary for its report row: the file count, then the files
       themselves with the line the Use sits on.

       CAPPED AT EIGHT, and it says so when it caps. A workspace can Use a library from a hundred
       files and the row is not the place to list them - but a silent "..." would read as the whole
       answer, and the count above it is what the user is actually deciding on. (No silent caps: the
       same rule the Deep Scan's own reporting follows.)

       Counted by FILE, not by Use: a file naming three of a library's packages is one file to open
       and one file to fix, and reporting 3 would inflate every number the user reasons with. */
    /* A path as a "show me" link: reveals it in Windows Explorer, dialog stays up.

       & and < are escaped because a path is DATA here, not markup, and it lands in an HTML
       attribute. "R&D" is a perfectly ordinary folder name and an unescaped & would silently
       truncate the link - the sort of thing that works on every machine but the one that has it. */
    Function PathLinkHtml String sPath String sLabel String sCls Returns String
        String sSafe

        Move (Replaces("&", sPath, "&amp;")) to sSafe
        Move (Replaces("<", sSafe, "&lt;"))  to sSafe
        Move (Replaces("&", sLabel, "&amp;")) to sLabel
        Move (Replaces("<", sLabel, "&lt;"))  to sLabel
        Function_Return ((SpanOpenClick(("ml-plink" * sCls), ("cfmdir:" + sSafe), "", "Show in Windows Explorer")) ;
            + sLabel + CS_SpanEnd)
    End_Function

    /* The nearest ancestor of sPath that actually EXISTS, or "" if none does.

       RDCRevealInExplorer refuses a path that is not on disk - a deliberate guard so a dialog body
       cannot launch something - which means a link to a MISSING file silently does nothing. That is
       the wrong behaviour for a report whose entire subject is files that are absent: the useful
       answer is "here is how far down the path does exist", because seeing that
       E:\LEGACYAPP6\Libraries is missing while E:\LEGACYAPP6 is fine is the whole diagnosis.
       Nils 2026-08-05. */
    Function NearestExistingFolder String sPath Returns String
        String sTry
        Integer iPos iGuard

        Move sPath to sTry
        If (Right(sTry, 1) = "\") ;
            Move (Left(sTry, Length(sTry) - 1)) to sTry
        Move 0 to iGuard
        While (sTry <> "" and iGuard < 64)
            If (vFolderExists(sTry) <> 0) ;
                Function_Return sTry
            If (vFilePathExists(sTry) <> 0) ;
                Function_Return sTry
            Move (RightPos("\", sTry)) to iPos
            If (iPos = 0) Begin
                Move "" to sTry
            End
            Else Begin
                Move (Left(sTry, iPos - 1)) to sTry
                // "C:" is not a folder - restore the separator so the drive root is testable.
                If (Right(sTry, 1) = ":") ;
                    Move (sTry + "\") to sTry
            End
            Increment iGuard
        Loop
        Function_Return ""
    End_Function

    Function UsageCellHtml String sLibName Returns String
        tLibraryUsage[] aUse
        String[] asSeen
        String sHtml sBase sLow sLink
        Integer i iMax iPos iFiles iShown

        Get pUsages to aUse
        Move (SizeOfArray(aUse) - 1) to iMax
        Move 0 to iFiles
        Move 0 to iShown
        Move "" to sHtml
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (aUse[i].sLibName = sLibName) Begin
                Move (Lowercase(aUse[i].sFile)) to sLow
                If ((SearchArray(sLow, asSeen)) = -1) Begin
                    Move sLow to asSeen[-1]
                    Increment iFiles
                    If (iShown < 8) Begin
                        Move (RightPos("\", aUse[i].sFile)) to iPos
                        If (iPos > 0) ;
                            Move (Mid(aUse[i].sFile, (Length(aUse[i].sFile) - iPos), (iPos + 1))) to sBase
                        Else ;
                            Move aUse[i].sFile to sBase
                        Get PathLinkHtml aUse[i].sFile sBase "ml-plink-file" to sLink
                        Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-use ml-use-file")) + sLink ;
                              + ' ' + (SpanOpen("ml-use ml-use-line")) + 'line ' + String(aUse[i].iLine) + (CS_SpanEnd + CS_DivEnd)) to sHtml
                        Increment iShown
                    End
                End
            End
        Loop
        If (iFiles = 0) ;
            Function_Return ((DivOpen("ml-use ml-use-none")) + 'used by no file directly' + CS_DivEnd)
        If (iFiles > iShown) ;
            Move (sHtml + (DivOpen("ml-use ml-use-more")) + 'and ' + String(iFiles - iShown) + (' more' + CS_DivEnd)) to sHtml
        Function_Return ((DivOpen("ml-use ml-use-head")) + 'used directly by ' ;
                         + String(iFiles) + ' file' + (If(iFiles = 1, "", "s")) + CS_DivEnd + sHtml)
    End_Function

    Function ScanLibraryUsages tLibraryInfo[] ByRef aLibs Boolean ByRef bCancelled Returns tLibraryUsage[]
        tLibraryUsage[] aUse
        tLibraryUsage   U
        tRefactorFiles  RFiles
        String[]        asFolders asLines asLibFolders

        String[][]      aasPkgs
        String          sFile sLow sPkg
        Handle          ho hoStatusBar
        Integer         iLib iLibMax iFile iFileLast iLine iLineMax iCheck i
        Boolean         bPaint bMine

        Move False to bCancelled
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iLibMax
        If (iLibMax < 0) ;
            Function_Return aUse

        // One package-name set per library, plus its folder for the exclusion test below.
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to ho
        For iLib from 0 to iLibMax
            Get LibraryPackageNames of ho aLibs[iLib].sFolder to aasPkgs[iLib]
            Move (Lowercase(vFolderFormat(aLibs[iLib].sFolder))) to asLibFolders[iLib]
        Loop
        Send Destroy of ho

        // Scope: the user's selected folders, else the whole workspace - the same resolution every
        // guided action uses, so "usages" means the same set of files as everything else on the board.
        Get pasSelectedFolders of ghoApplication to asFolders
        If (SizeOfArray(asFolders) = 0) ;
            Get WholeWorkspaceSourceFolders of ghoApplication to asFolders
        If (SizeOfArray(asFolders) = 0) ;
            Function_Return aUse
        Get AllSourceFiles of ghoApplication asFolders SysFile.FileExtensionFilter to RFiles
        Move (SizeOfArray(RFiles.asFullFileNames) - 1) to iFileLast
        If (iFileLast < 0) ;
            Function_Return aUse

        Get Statusbar_Id of (phoMainPanel(ghoApplication)) to hoStatusBar
        Send Cursor_Wait of Cursor_Control
        Send StartStatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel "Libraries - finding which files use them..." "" (iFileLast + 1) (iFileLast + 1)
        For iFile from 0 to iFileLast
            Move RFiles.asFullFileNames[iFile] to sFile
            Move (Lowercase(sFile)) to sLow
            // A file INSIDE a library is that library's own business, not a usage of it.
            Move False to bMine
            For i from 0 to iLibMax
                If (asLibFolders[i] <> "" and (Pos(asLibFolders[i], sLow)) = 1) ;
                    Move True to bMine
            Loop
            If (bMine = False) Begin
                Move (rfReadEncodedFileAsStringArray(sFile)) to asLines
                Move (SizeOfArray(asLines) - 1) to iLineMax
                Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to ho
                For iLine from 0 to iLineMax
                    Get UsedPackageOnLine of ho asLines[iLine] to sPkg
                    If (sPkg <> "") Begin
                        For iLib from 0 to iLibMax
                            If ((SearchArray(sPkg, aasPkgs[iLib])) <> -1) Begin
                                Move aLibs[iLib].sName to U.sLibName
                                Move sFile             to U.sFile
                                Move (iLine + 1)       to U.iLine
                                Move sPkg              to U.sPackage
                                Move U to aUse[-1]
                            End
                        Loop
                    End
                Loop
                Send Destroy of ho
            End
            // Same time-driven paint + cancel gate as the Deep Scan (see cFTDeepScanDriver): the
            // per-file work here is a read and a line walk, so paying four message pumps per file
            // would cost more than the scan.
            Get PanelPaintDue of ghoStatusPanel to bPaint
            If (bPaint = True) Begin
                Set Caption_Text of ghoStatusPanel to ("Libraries - File No:" * String(iFile + 1) * "of:" * String(iFileLast + 1))
                Set TableName_Text of ghoStatusPanel to sFile
                Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to ("Finding library usages: file " + String(iFile + 1) + " of " + String(iFileLast + 1))
                Send PumpMsgQueue of Desktop
            End
            Set piPositionTot of ghoStatusPanel to (iFile + 1)
            Move 0 to iCheck
            If (bPaint = True) ;
                Get Check_StatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel to iCheck
            If (iCheck = MSG_CANCEL) Begin
                Move True to bCancelled
                Move (ResizeArray(aUse, 0)) to aUse
                Send Cursor_Ready of Cursor_Control
                Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to "Ready."
                Function_Return aUse
            End
        Loop
        Send Stop_StatusPanel of ghoStatusPanel
        Send Cursor_Ready of Cursor_Control
        Set StatusText of hoStatusBar to "Ready."
        Function_Return aUse
    End_Function

    /* THE LIBRARIES TILE: the libraries this workspace uses, and whether they can meet the target.

       ITS OWN TILE since 2026-09-10 (Nils: "three tiles with a gap"). It was the right HALF of the
       Target tile, divided by a rule, because a library's verdict is a consequence of the target
       version rather than a peer setting. That reading has not changed - it still sits immediately
       right of Target so the dependency reads left to right, and wears that tile's exact gold - but
       a half of a half had no room to say a second, independent thing, and the row levels its
       children now (.dash-cols), which it could not when the halves were merged.

       The sub-line is the whole point: a bare "Libraries" says nothing, while "2 of 5 need a 26.0
       build" is the answer without opening anything. */
    { Visibility=Private }
    Function LibraryTileHtml Returns String
        String sWS sTarget sSub sSkin sHas sUpd sStale
        Integer iBehind iStale
        Boolean bAnswered
        Integer iTotal iAttn iPkgs
        Boolean bJson

        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget
        Move "" to sSkin
        If (sWS = "") Begin
            Move "Load a workspace to see them" to sSub
        End
        Else Begin
            Send RefreshLibraryCounts
            Get piLibTotal to iTotal
            Get piLibAttention to iAttn
            Get piLibPackages to iPkgs
            Get pbLibJsonForm to bJson

            /* WHAT THE WORKSPACE HAS, in one phrase - "2 libraries and 3 packages". Built first
               because three of the four branches below want it. Singular forms because "1 librarys"
               in a dashboard reads as a bug in everything else too. */
            Move "" to sHas
            If (iTotal > 0) ;
                Move (String(iTotal) + (If(iTotal = 1, " library", " libraries"))) to sHas
            If (iPkgs > 0) Begin
                If (sHas <> "") ;
                    Move (sHas + " and ") to sHas
                Move (sHas + String(iPkgs) + (If(iPkgs = 1, " package", " packages"))) to sHas
            End

            If (iTotal = 0 and iPkgs = 0) Begin
                Move "None declared by this workspace" to sSub
            End
            Else If (iTotal = 0) Begin
                // Packages only: nothing here carries a .sws version, so there is no verdict to
                // give and none is implied. Not an attention state - it is a normal DF26 workspace.
                Move sHas to sSub
                Move " ok" to sSkin
            End
            Else If (sTarget = "") Begin
                Move (sHas + " " + CS_EmDash + " pick a compiler for a verdict") to sSub
            End
            Else If (iAttn > 0) Begin
                /* THE DENOMINATOR IS iTotal, NOT iTotal + iPkgs. A package pins its own version and
                   has no .sws for this tile to judge, so counting it among the ones that "need a
                   build" would put it in a verdict nobody formed about it. */
                Move (String(iAttn) + " of " + String(iTotal) + " need a " + sTarget + " build") to sSub
                Move " attn" to sSkin
            End
            Else If (bJson = True) Begin
                /* NO VERSION TALK ON A DF26 WORKSPACE (Nils 2026-08-14: "the version is of no
                   interest as it is a DF 26 workspace"). Its dependencies are packages pinned by
                   commit and libraries the package handler resolved, so "all on 26.0" restates the
                   format rather than telling anyone anything. What it has is the useful fact; the
                   green skin already carries "and nothing needs attention". */
                Move sHas to sSub
                Move " ok" to sSkin
            End
            Else Begin
                // A legacy INI workspace still gets the version, where it is the whole question.
                Move ("All " + String(iTotal) + " are on " + sTarget) to sSub
                Move " ok" to sSkin
            End
        End
        /* THE SECOND LINE: what a remote says, and NOTHING when nothing said anything.

           Three states, not two. Behind is the actionable one and wears the warning colour;
           checked-and-current earns a quiet line, because a user whose workspace was just scanned
           deserves an answer rather than an unchanged-looking tile. The third state - no scan yet,
           no network, no git on PATH, a df-cli that would not read the workspace, or a workspace
           whose dependencies are every one of them local paths - renders NOTHING. Silence is
           honest; "up to date" would be a claim, and it would be made in exactly the cases where
           it is most likely to be false.

           "Package", not "library": a path entry has no remote to ask. Most entries in a legacy
           workspace are paths, so a line saying "libraries" would describe rows nothing looked at. */
        /* THE THIRD LINE: a library folder a package replaced, still on disk. Above the update
           line because it is the older and more misleading state - the build reads the package,
           so anything changed in these folders never reaches it. Silent when there are none,
           which is every tidy workspace. */
        Move "" to sStale
        If (sWS <> "") Begin
            Get piLibStale to iStale
            If (iStale > 0) ;
                Move ((DivOpen("dash-p dash-stale")) + String(iStale) ;
                    + (If(iStale = 1, " replaced library folder", " replaced library folders")) ;
                    + " on disk" + CS_DivEnd) to sStale
        End
        Move "" to sUpd
        If (sWS <> "" and (UpdateScanDone(Self, sWS)) = True) Begin
            Get pbUpdAnswered to bAnswered
            Get piUpdBehind   to iBehind
            If (bAnswered = True) Begin
                If (iBehind > 0) ;
                    Move ((DivOpen("dash-p dash-upd-behind")) + String(iBehind) ;
                        + (If(iBehind = 1, " package needs", " packages need")) + " an update" + CS_DivEnd) to sUpd
                Else ;
                    Move ((DivOpen("dash-p dash-upd-ok")) + "Packages up to date" + CS_DivEnd) to sUpd
            End
        End
        Function_Return ((ClickableHtml("div", ('dash-tile t-libs' + sSkin), "libraries", "", ((DivOpen("dash-ico")) + (DashIcon(CS_GlyphStack))), "The shared libraries this workspace uses, and whether each has a build for the target DataFlex version. Library code is maintained upstream and is never refactored in place - it is replaced.")) + (((DivOpen("dash-txt")) + (DivOpen("dash-h")) + 'Libraries') + CS_DivEnd + (DivOpen("dash-p"))) + sSub + CS_DivEnd + sStale + sUpd + (CS_DivEnd + CS_DivEnd))
    End_Function

    /* Counts for the tile half, memoised on (workspace + target version).

       The inventory does a directory search plus a read of every sibling .sws per library - about
       30 small file reads for 5 libraries - and the tile re-renders on every dashboard entry, every
       workspace load and after every compiler change. Recomputing each time is exactly the shape of
       the pinned-indicator regression (24s of a 55s Analyze), so it is computed once per
       (workspace, target) pair and the key self-invalidates when either changes. */
    { Visibility=Private }
    Procedure RefreshLibraryCounts
        tLibraryInfo[] aLibs
        String[] asSeenLibs
        Handle ho
        String sWS sTarget sKey sSeen sSeenKey
        Integer i iMax iAttn iTotal iPkgs iFormat
        tSwsDependency[] aPkgGraph
        String[] asPkgVia
        Integer[] aiPkgDepth
        String sPkgHome

        Get psSWSFile of ghoApplication to sWS
        Get psTargetDfVersion of ghoApplication to sTarget
        /* THE FILE'S OWN STAMP IS PART OF THE KEY (Nils 2026-08-15: after retargeting five
           libraries to 26.0 and pressing OK, the tile still said "8 of 8 need a 26.0 build").
           The memo keyed on (workspace + target) alone, and neither changes when the .sws is
           EDITED - so a Libraries-dialog OK, which rewrites the very file these counts describe,
           left the tile serving the pre-edit numbers forever. rfFileDate is one FindFirstFile,
           which is nothing next to the tree walk it guards; a rewrite changes the stamp and the
           memo misses on the next paint, as it must. */
        Move (Lowercase(sWS) + "|" + sTarget + "|" + String(rfFileDate(sWS))) to sKey
        Get psLibCountKey to sSeen
        If (sKey = sSeen) ;
            Procedure_Return

        /* WorkspaceLibraryEntries, NOT WorkspaceLibraryInventory (2026-08-11). The inventory keeps
           only entries whose .sws resolves, so a workspace declaring six libraries with two missing
           painted "All 4 are on 26.0" - a green tile over a workspace that will not build, and a
           total that silently disagreed with the file. A missing entry is now counted, and counted
           as needing attention: it is the most actionable state a row can be in. */
        /* THE TREE, so a broken CHAIN reaches the tile (2026-08-12). A library's Version= describes
           itself and not what it depends on, so WebAppSrc-Library-DF250 counted as one tidy library
           on E:\WinServ while the seven it declares sit on 19.1 - the tile was green over a
           workspace that cannot build. A library whose own dependencies undercut it now counts as
           needing attention.

           THE TOTAL STAYS THE WORKSPACE'S OWN count. The tile reports what this workspace declares
           - which is what the user edits - and the chain sharpens the attention half rather than
           inflating the denominator. The memo keeps the extra reads off the paint path. */
        Get Create (RefClass(cLibraryInventoryApp)) to ho
        Get WorkspaceLibraryTree of ho sWS sTarget CI_LibraryTreeMaxDepth to aLibs
        /* PACKAGES ARE LIBRARIES TOO, and the tree cannot see them (Nils 2026-08-14: "the number of
           libraries + packages is far more than 1").

           WorkspaceLibraryTree is built from SwsLibraryEntries, which reports only the STRING
           dependencies - a git or cloud package is an object with no path to resolve, so it never
           reaches the tree. On his DF26 WinServ that left one path entry and two git packages, and
           the tile said "All 1". The same reader/writer asymmetry that made the JSON writer delete
           packages made this tile fail to count them. */
        /* THE WHOLE GRAPH, not just the entries this workspace names (Nils 2026-08-21, on a
           tile reading "1 package" beside a dialog listing four).

           SwsJsonObjectDependencyCount answers the DIRECT count, which is what the .sws
           records - but a package brings its own dependencies with it and those are installed
           too. Order Entry names DUF alone and ends up with DUF, RDCToolsLib, DFAbout and
           vWin32fh. Counting only the named one repeats the mistake this file already fixed
           for LIBRARIES: a number that matches nothing else on screen.

           ExpandPackageGraph is the SAME walk the Libraries dialog lists from, deliberately -
           a second copy of that loop is how the tile and the dialog drift apart again. The
           home folder is the .sws's own, which is where DfPkg sits. */
        Move (Left(sWS, RightPos("\", sWS))) to sPkgHome
        Get ExpandPackageGraph of ho sWS sPkgHome (&aPkgGraph) (&asPkgVia) (&aiPkgDepth) to iPkgs
        Get SwsFormatOf of ho sWS to iFormat
        Send Destroy of ho
        /* EVERY LIBRARY IN THE CHAIN, not just the ones this workspace names directly
           (Nils 2026-08-14: "one major library + four nested libraries = 5 + 2 packages").

           The total used to be the workspace's OWN entries, on the reasoning that those are what the
           user edits, with a nested problem folded into its parent through iChainBehind. On his
           WinServ that produced "1" over a workspace carrying five libraries and two packages - a
           number that matched no other count on screen, least of all the Libraries dialog's own list.
           What the workspace COMPILES is the honest measure, and it is the one the dialog shows.

           Counting every depth also retires the iChainBehind roll-up here: a late nested library now
           counts once, as itself, instead of once as its parent's chain flag. Adding both would have
           counted the same problem twice.

           Deduplicated on the RESOLVED .sws, because a library reached through two parents is one
           library - and a workspace that (like his did) references two versions of the same library
           pulls in two separate chains, which would otherwise inflate the total with rows the user
           would count once. */
        Move 0 to iAttn
        Move 0 to iTotal
        Move (SizeOfArray(aLibs) - 1) to iMax
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Move (Lowercase(Trim(aLibs[i].sRefSwsFile))) to sSeenKey
            If (sSeenKey = "") ;
                Move (Lowercase(Trim(aLibs[i].sName)) + "|" + String(i)) to sSeenKey
            If (SearchArray(sSeenKey, asSeenLibs) = -1) Begin
                Move sSeenKey to asSeenLibs[-1]
                Increment iTotal
                /* THE SAME RULE THE DIALOG APPLIES (MaintainLibrariesFT.AttentionCount, 2026-08-15):
                   on a DF26 JSON workspace a version verdict is not formed - each dependency pins
                   its own version - so only what is broken in ANY format counts: not-found, and a
                   package that itself refuses the target. Without this the tile said "8 of 8 need
                   a 26.0 build" over eight libraries that need nothing, and disagreed with the
                   dialog it opens. INI workspaces keep the full verdict; there it is the question. */
                If (iFormat = eSwsJson) Begin
                    If (aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibNotFound or aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibUnreadable or aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibPackageMismatch) ;
                        Increment iAttn
                End
                Else If (aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibRetargetLocal or aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibNoLocalBuild or aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibNotFound or aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibUnreadable or aLibs[i].eStatus = eLibPackageMismatch) ;
                    Increment iAttn
            End
        Loop
        Set piLibTotal     to iTotal
        Set piLibAttention to iAttn
        Set piLibPackages  to iPkgs
        Set pbLibJsonForm  to (iFormat = eSwsJson)
        Send RescanStaleLibraryFolders
        Set psLibCountKey  to sKey
    End_Function

    /* Re-reads which <home>\Libraries children a package has replaced and nothing declares, and
       stores the count and the folders. Called from RefreshLibraryCounts (so the tile pays for it
       once per workspace, on the same memo) and again by the Libraries dialog after a retire.

       CHEAP WHEN THERE IS NOTHING: no Libraries folder, no walk at all - which is every workspace
       that never had one, and every one already tidied. It only reaches the file system for the
       few folders that are both undeclared and matched to a package. */
    Procedure RescanStaleLibraryFolders
        Handle ho
        String[] asFolder asPkg asDeclared
        Integer[] aiSame aiDiffer aiOnly
        String sHome sSws
        Integer iN

        Move 0 to iN
        Get psHomeTarget of ghoApplication to sHome
        Get psSWSFile    of ghoApplication to sSws
        /* THE DECLARED SET COMES FROM THE RESOLVER, never from the .sws text: a git dependency's
           URL names the library it replaced, so a "does the .sws mention it" test would call every
           replaced folder declared for ever. GetWorkspaceLibrarySourceFolders follows local
           dependencies and skips git ones, which is exactly the distinction wanted. */
        If (sSws <> "") ;
            Get GetWorkspaceLibrarySourceFolders of ghoApplication sSws to asDeclared
        Get Create (RefClass(cShadowedPackageFiles)) to ho
        If (ho <> 0) Begin
            Get OrphanedLibraryFolders of ho sHome asDeclared (&asFolder) (&asPkg) ;
                (&aiSame) (&aiDiffer) (&aiOnly) to iN
            Send Destroy of ho
        End
        Set pasLibStaleFolder to asFolder
        Set piLibStale        to iN
    End_Procedure

    /* How many library folders a package has replaced, for a caller outside this object - the
       Libraries dialog's Retire folders button. Goes through the MEMO, so asking on every repaint
       of that dialog costs one string compare; RefreshLibraryCounts stays private. */
    Function StaleLibraryFolderCount Returns Integer
        Send RefreshLibraryCounts
        Function_Return (piLibStale(Self))
    End_Function

    // Forces the next tile paint - and the next read of piLibStale - to re-scan. Sent after a
    // retire, which changes the answer without touching the .sws the memo key watches.
    Procedure InvalidateLibraryCounts
        Set psLibCountKey to ""
    End_Procedure


    /* Compiler messages per library, tallied from the analyzer's own scoped message set.

       This is the other half of the same fact: "vwin32fh has no 26.0 build" and "47 messages inside
       vwin32fh files" are one problem stated twice, and the report is where they meet. The rows stay
       in the grid - nothing is hidden, and their count is what makes the library row worth reading.

       Returns False when the analyzer has no data, which the caller must distinguish from a genuine
       zero: "no messages" and "not analyzed yet" look identical in a count and mean opposite things.

       paScopedMessages is the analyzer's single parsed set, already read cross-object this way by
       the plan builder (StartAutoSelect). */
    // No longer private: the Library Maintenance dialog is the screen that renders these now
    // (cFTLibraryReport kept the DATA when the report moved into a real dialog, 2026-08-11).
    Function LibraryMessageCounts String[] ByRef asNames Integer[] ByRef aiCounts Returns Boolean
        tCompileError[] aMsgs
        Handle hoAn
        String sLib
        Integer i iMax iAt

        If (ghoModAnalyzerDock = 0) ;
            Function_Return False
        Get paScopedMessages of (oModAnalyzer_grd(ghoModAnalyzerDock)) to aMsgs
        Move (SizeOfArray(aMsgs) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax < 0) ;
            Function_Return False

        Get Create (RefClass(cModAnalyzer)) to hoAn
        For i from 0 to iMax
            Get LibraryOwningFile of hoAn aMsgs[i].sFileName to sLib
            If (sLib <> "") Begin
                Move (SearchArray(sLib, asNames)) to iAt
                If (iAt = -1) Begin
                    Move sLib to asNames[-1]
                    Move 1 to aiCounts[-1]
                End
                Else ;
                    Move (aiCounts[iAt] + 1) to aiCounts[iAt]
            End
        Loop
        Send Destroy of hoAn
        Function_Return True
    End_Function

    // "19.1, 25.0" - the versions a library copy offers, or "-" when it declares none.
    // Not private, same reason as LibraryMessageCounts above - the dialog renders this column.
    Function _VersionListText String[] ByRef asVers Returns String
        String sOut
        Integer i iMax
        Move (SizeOfArray(asVers) - 1) to iMax
        If (iMax < 0) ;
            Function_Return "-"
        Move "" to sOut
        For i from 0 to iMax
            If (sOut <> "") ;
                Move (sOut + ", ") to sOut
            Move (sOut + asVers[i]) to sOut
        Loop
        Function_Return sOut
    End_Function
End_Class

// One instance. Both the Dashboard and the guided trail call through this handle.
#IFNDEF ghoLibraryReport
    Global_Variable Handle ghoLibraryReport
    Move 0 to ghoLibraryReport
#ENDIF

Object oLibraryReport is a cFTLibraryReport
    Move Self to ghoLibraryReport
End_Object
